<p class="gna-lede">Dermal fillers are gel-like substances injected beneath the skin to restore lost volume, soften folds or reshape areas such as the lips, cheeks and chin. The most widely used fillers are made of hyaluronic acid, typically last somewhere between six and eighteen months depending on the product and area, and can be dissolved if needed. They are generally low-risk in trained hands, but they are a medical procedure: rare complications such as a blocked blood vessel can be serious, which is why who injects you matters as much as what is injected.</p>

<h2 id="what-are-fillers">What are dermal fillers?</h2>
<p>As we age, the face gradually loses fat, bone support and collagen. Cheeks flatten, the folds running from the nose to the mouth deepen, the lips thin and the jawline softens. Fillers replace some of that lost volume or add structure where it is wanted.</p>
<p>Fillers are different from botulinum toxin, often called by the brand name Botox. Toxin relaxes the muscles that cause <em>dynamic</em> lines — frown lines, forehead lines and crow's feet — that appear with expression. Fillers treat <em>static</em> folds and volume loss that are visible even when the face is at rest. Some people benefit from one, some from the other and some from a combination; our page on <a href="/botox-treatment">Botox treatment</a> explains the toxin side.</p>
<p>Common treatment areas include the lips, the folds around the mouth (nasolabial folds and marionette lines), the cheeks, the chin and jawline, the temples and the hollows under the eyes (tear troughs). Some areas — the nose, the space between the eyebrows and the tear troughs in particular — carry higher risks and demand particular skill and anatomical knowledge.</p>

<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Hyaluronic acid (HA) fillers are the most common type and can be dissolved with an enzyme called hyaluronidase.</li>
    <li>Results are temporary and vary — typically six to eighteen months, depending on the product, area and individual.</li>
    <li>Bruising and swelling are common; vascular occlusion is rare but is an emergency.</li>
    <li>Choose a registered medical or dental professional trained in facial anatomy who can manage complications.</li>
    <li>Fillers do not lift sagging skin; for significant laxity, surgery may be the more appropriate option.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="types">Types of filler compared</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Filler type</th><th>How it works</th><th>Typical duration</th><th>Can it be dissolved?</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Hyaluronic acid (HA)</td><td>A gel made from a sugar molecule found naturally in skin; adds volume and attracts water</td><td>About 6–18 months, depending on product and area</td><td>Yes, with hyaluronidase</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Calcium hydroxylapatite (CaHA)</td><td>Mineral microspheres in a gel; adds volume and stimulates collagen</td><td>About 12 months or more</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Poly-L-lactic acid (PLLA)</td><td>Stimulates the body's own collagen gradually over a course of sessions</td><td>Up to about two years</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Non-absorbable fillers (e.g. PMMA)</td><td>Tiny beads that remain in the tissue indefinitely</td><td>Long-lasting</td><td>No; complications may need surgery</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Fat transfer</td><td>Your own fat, harvested by liposuction and re-injected</td><td>Variable; part of the fat may persist long term</td><td>No</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>For most first-time patients, a hyaluronic acid filler is the usual recommendation because its effect is temporary and reversible. Non-absorbable fillers are used far less often because, if a problem develops years later, it can be difficult to treat.</p>

<h2 id="how-long">How long do fillers last?</h2>
<p>Duration varies more than many advertisements suggest. It depends on:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The product</strong> — firmer, more cross-linked HA gels designed for the cheeks and chin tend to last longer than softer gels used in the lips.</li>
  <li><strong>The area</strong> — the lips and the skin around the mouth move constantly and usually break filler down faster, often within six to nine months; deeper placement over bone may last a year or longer.</li>
  <li><strong>Your body</strong> — metabolism, age, smoking, sun exposure and intense exercise all play a part.</li>
  <li><strong>The amount used</strong> and whether you have had treatment before.</li>
</ul>
<p>Filler can also remain in the tissue for longer than its visible effect, sometimes for years. Repeating treatment before the previous filler has broken down can gradually add bulk and produce a heavy, unnatural look. A conservative approach — less product, reviewed over time — usually ages better.</p>

<h2 id="procedure">What to expect on the day and afterwards</h2>
<h3>Consultation</h3>
<p>A proper consultation comes first, ideally on a separate day from treatment. Your practitioner should ask about your medical history, medicines (including blood thinners and supplements that increase bruising), allergies, previous fillers or facial surgery, and cold sores. They should examine your face at rest and in movement, discuss what is realistically achievable and explain the risks before you sign a consent form. Clinical photographs, if taken, form part of your medical record and should be taken and stored only with your consent.</p>
<h3>The procedure</h3>
<p>The skin is cleaned and a numbing cream is often applied; many fillers also contain a local anaesthetic. The filler is placed with a fine needle or a blunt-tipped cannula. Treatment usually takes 15 to 45 minutes. You will feel pressure and some discomfort, which most people tolerate well.</p>
<h3>Afterwards</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Swelling, redness, tenderness and bruising are common for several days; lips can swell noticeably for the first 48 hours.</li>
  <li>Small lumps can often be felt for a week or two as the filler settles.</li>
  <li>For a day or two, avoid strenuous exercise, alcohol, saunas and firm pressure on the treated area unless advised otherwise.</li>
  <li>A review about two weeks later allows any small adjustments once the swelling has gone down.</li>
</ul>
<p>Most people return to work the same or the next day, although bruising may take a week or more to fade — worth considering before a wedding or a major event.</p>

