<h2 id="risks">Risks, including vascular occlusion</h2>
<p>Most side effects are mild and temporary: bruising, swelling, tenderness, redness, asymmetry and small lumps. Less common problems include infection, cold-sore flare-ups, allergic reactions, filler moving away from where it was placed, a bluish tinge when filler sits too close to the surface under the eyes, and firm nodules that can appear weeks or months later.</p>
<h3>Vascular occlusion: the complication everyone should know about</h3>
<p>Rarely, filler is injected into a blood vessel or compresses one, blocking blood flow to the skin. If this is not treated quickly, the skin can break down and scar. Very rarely, filler travels into the arteries supplying the eye and causes sudden, lasting loss of vision, or affects blood flow to the brain. The area between the eyebrows, the nose, the nasolabial folds and the forehead are among the higher-risk sites.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Contact your practitioner immediately — and go to an emergency department or call 108 if you cannot reach them — if during or after filler treatment you notice:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Whitening (blanching) of the skin, or a mottled, lacy, purple or dusky patch</li>
    <li>Severe pain that is out of proportion to what you expected, or is getting worse</li>
    <li>Skin that feels cool, or blisters or scabs appearing over the following days</li>
    <li>Any change in vision, eye pain, double vision or a drooping eyelid</li>
    <li>Weakness, numbness, difficulty speaking or confusion</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Visual symptoms are an emergency in which minutes matter.</p>
</div>
<p>Your practitioner should have an emergency protocol and hyaluronidase available on site for exactly this reason. Ask about it before treatment — a clear, specific answer is a good sign.</p>

<h2 id="reversibility">Can fillers be dissolved?</h2>
<p>Hyaluronic acid fillers can be broken down by an enzyme called <strong>hyaluronidase</strong>, injected into the treated area. It is used to correct overfilling, lumps, asymmetry or filler that has moved, and — in larger, repeated doses — as emergency treatment for vascular occlusion.</p>
<p>Hyaluronidase works within hours to days, but it has drawbacks. It can cause swelling and, uncommonly, allergic reactions, so some practitioners perform a small skin test first when time allows. It may also temporarily break down some of your own natural hyaluronic acid, and more than one session is sometimes needed. Dissolving and re-treating should be a considered decision, not a routine cycle.</p>
<p>Calcium hydroxylapatite, poly-L-lactic acid and non-absorbable fillers cannot be dissolved this way. Problems with these may need steroid injections, other medicines or surgical removal — another reason to know exactly which product is being used.</p>

<h2 id="choosing-practitioner">How to choose a qualified practitioner</h2>
<p>Filler injection is a medical procedure, yet it is widely offered in salons, spas and at home visits, sometimes by people without medical training. Before you book, check that:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The injector is a <strong>registered medical or dental professional</strong> with specific training in facial anatomy and aesthetic injection, and is happy to tell you their qualifications.</li>
  <li>Treatment takes place in a <strong>clinical setting</strong> with proper hygiene, not at home or at a party.</li>
  <li>You are told the <strong>exact product name</strong>, and it comes from a sealed, branded pack approved for use in India. Ask for the product label or batch number to be recorded in your notes.</li>
  <li>The practitioner keeps <strong>hyaluronidase and an emergency plan</strong> on site and can be contacted after treatment.</li>
  <li>You receive a <strong>full consultation, an honest discussion of risks and written consent</strong>, with time to think rather than pressure to decide on the day.</li>
  <li>They are prepared to say <strong>no</strong> if filler is not right for you.</li>
</ul>
<p>Avoid buying filler online or using "needle-free" hyaluronic pens on yourself. Products of unknown origin and untrained technique lie behind many serious complications. The NHS guidance on choosing who will do your cosmetic procedure, listed in the references below, offers a useful checklist.</p>

<h2 id="is-filler-right">Are fillers right for you?</h2>
<p>Fillers work well for mild to moderate volume loss, thin lips, early folds and contour changes in the chin, jawline and cheeks. They are less suitable when the main problem is loose, sagging skin of the lower face and neck — adding filler to sagging tissue tends to add weight without lift. In that situation a surgical option such as a <a href="/blogs/face-lift-surgery-types-recovery-expectations/">face lift</a> may give a more natural result. If drooping brows are your concern, see our comparison of <a href="/blogs/brow-lift-vs-botox-droopy-brows/">brow lift and Botox</a>.</p>
<p>It also helps to be clear about your goals. Fillers can refresh and balance the face; they cannot stop ageing, and results vary from person to person. If you feel intense distress about a feature that others barely notice, it is worth discussing that openly before any cosmetic procedure.</p>
